// Issue #229 regression: a constructor call must supply exactly the variant's
// declared fields. The shape is known where the variant is declared, so this
// is checkable in unannotated code — before this, Point(1, 2) for a
// three-field variant built a value that a Point(a, b, c) pattern silently
// missed (match fell through to nil), and the mistake surfaced far from its
// cause.
//
// The desugar is (cons (quote Point) args), so a constructor is not a curried
// function: Point(1) is not a partially applied constructor, it is a
// malformed pair. Arity is therefore exact in both directions, exactly like
// the builtin arity check (same [E0003], see builtin-arity-errors.ta).
pub type Point { Point(x, y, z) }

// Issue #229 follow-up: a `name : type` label on a variant field is
// documentation, not a field — the fields of R are `int` and `int`
// (see docs/ta-language-spec.md「ADT」). Labels used to be mis-parsed into
// extra nil fields, which is why the arity here is 2 and not 6.
pub type Rational { R(num : int, den : int) }

pub type Shape { Circle(r) Dot }

fn too_few() { Point(1, 2) }

fn labelled_too_few() { R(1) }

fn too_many() { Circle(1, 2) }

fn nullary_with_args() { Dot(1) }

fn main() {
  print("unreachable")
}

// expect: wrong number of arguments for 'Point' (got 2, want 3)
// expect: wrong number of arguments for 'R' (got 1, want 2)